WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.420
Across series, galaxies, and generations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.500
one question has been left fairly&nbsp;
open-ended in the Star Trek franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.180
After over a half-century's worth of series&nbsp;
and films, we may finally have an answer.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:18.000
Starfleet has its fair share of heroes — but&nbsp;
unfortunately, few admirals fall into that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.920
category. Achieving a high rank in the Federation&nbsp;
might be a major reward for the intergalactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.780
explorers, but the price of having such a&nbsp;
powerful role has corrupted more than one leader.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.460
Viewers have long argued about these leaders&nbsp;
and their various levels of failure. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.500
the popularity of Star Trek: Picard seemingly&nbsp;
reinvigorating the franchise, it only makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.480
that the discussion is popping up once again. Many&nbsp;
have questioned what causes admirals to go bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.700
and this discourse has opened up a theory that has&nbsp;
long followed the franchise across various series.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.880
After the original series,&nbsp;
its follow-ups, Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.160
The Next Generation and Star Trek: Deep Space&nbsp;
Nine, began to explore the darker side of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.880
using admirals as a catalyst&nbsp;
for some pretty bleak plotlines.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.800
In Star Trek: The Next Generation in particular,&nbsp;
it seems that the admirals are really only there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.380
to bother crew members, specifically captains.&nbsp;
Despite Picard's loyal service to Starfleet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.880
they constantly question the captain's choices.&nbsp;
For someone who has been entrusted to run the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.660
Enterprise, it's surprising how little&nbsp;
faith Starfleet's leaders have in him.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.320
It's hard to overlook "The Drumhead"&nbsp;
when bringing up questionable admirals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.240
where a dilithium chamber explosion leads&nbsp;
to accusations of sabotage. In response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.400
a retired admiral — Norah Satie heads up an&nbsp;
investigation on the Enterprise. The crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.600
discovers it was not a deliberate act,&nbsp;
but Satie refuses to stop her pursuit.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.500
Her eventual questioning of even&nbsp;
Picard himself leads to a tribunal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.680
and the entire storyline exposes a fear, hatred,&nbsp;
and paranoia that continues to be relevant in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.120
today's society. Seeing Satie confronted&nbsp;
with the words of her father provides a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.040
powerful rebuttal to such loathsome qualities&nbsp;
and shows how important it is to stop them.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.380
"The first speech censured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:04.380
the first thought forbidden, the first&nbsp;
freedom denied chains us all irrevocably."

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.840
In turn, Deep Space Nine offered Admiral Ross,&nbsp;
whose motives have led to healthy debates among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.900
fans. One Redditor praised Ross for not occupying&nbsp;
the archetypal, quote, "Starfleet Badmiral," role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.560
in the series, while others were able&nbsp;
to identify acts like allying himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.200
to the morally ambiguous Section 31 as fairly&nbsp;
destructive, even if it benefited Starfleet.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.480
The Star Trek franchise crossed over into adult&nbsp;
animation with the series Star Trek: Lower Decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.700
It follows crew members of the ship Cerritos,&nbsp;
giving them an unprecedented active voice. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.300
many live-action series, these characters&nbsp;
would have been relegated to the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:45.000
or completely unseen in many cases, providing&nbsp;
a unique perspective on Starfleet goings on.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.200
Tackling overlooked details also gave&nbsp;
the series a chance to reflect on why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.480
admirals often turn bad. In one episode, Vice&nbsp;
Admiral Buenamigo even talks about reaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.680
the coveted level and demystifies&nbsp;
its importance. According to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.700
the lack of a reason to push forward sends&nbsp;
seemingly ethical leaders on the wrong path.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.500
"Star Fleet is so competitive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.000
Once you're an admiral, you hit a wall. I've&nbsp;
been trying for years to make a name for myself”

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.320
It doesn't hurt that Star Trek has often&nbsp;
explored the idea of admirals gone bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.480
The Season 3 finale of Lower Decks featured&nbsp;
Buenamigo coming to terms with his own decisions;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.440
an idea of AI in place of the Starfleet&nbsp;
crew may have seemed like a good idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.220
to Buenamigo at one point, but it&nbsp;
soon proved to be a deadly decision.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.720
Much like Picard and Admiral Satie,&nbsp;
the choice of efficiency over actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.040
crew experience and empathy touches&nbsp;
on an unfortunate pattern: higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.900
ranking officials demeaning or overlooking the&nbsp;
contributions of lower ranked Starfleet members.

